owner references: hold instance during Graph teardown via patch node (#203)

## Summary

- Compose two standard K8s primitives for ordered instance teardown:
`ownerReference` (GC cascade) + `patch:` node (finalizer blocking)
- Controller detects owner Terminating and self-deletes; teardown prunes
the patch, releasing the finalizer via SSA field release
- Zero new API surface — no new spec fields, no new node types. The
lifecycle coupling is expressed entirely through existing Graph
primitives

## Design

The RGD stdlib adds an `instanceLifecycle` patch node to per-instance
Graphs that places `experimental.kro.run/instance-lifecycle` on the
Instance. The controller's only new behavior is `ownerDeleting()` (~20
lines): detect owner's `deletionTimestamp` → self-delete. Everything
else uses existing mechanics:

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| Cascade (Graph dies when owner dies) | `ownerDeleting()` — detect
owner Terminating, self-delete |
| Blocking (Owner waits for teardown) | `patch:` node places finalizer
on owner |
| Ordered cleanup | Graph's own finalizer + existing teardown |
| Release owner | Patch pruned during teardown → SSA releases finalizer
|

## Teardown ordering fix

Patch field release now runs AFTER template deletion (was before). This
ensures lifecycle finalizers aren't released while managed resources
still exist. The ordering is correct in general: clean up your own
resources, then release claims on others.

## Tests

4 e2e tests validate the composition: finalizer placement via patch,
full teardown lifecycle, race condition (owner gone before Graph), and
ordering guarantee.
